            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Replaces each value with the smallest value seen up to that point: the running min. The result only ever falls or stays level - a trough hold.\n\nUse it for the lowest point reached so far, the best time so far, a floor that only ever drops. Applied to a whole recording at once, it shows at every moment the record so far.\n\nnan values are skipped: a gap does not reset the run.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.minimum.accumulate <axis: int>\nDefault: axis -1 (along each row).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is processed and sent.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way to run: -1 along each row, 0 down each column - one running min per joint for a buffer of frames x joints.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrunning min:\nThe smallest value so far at each position, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n32 random values (left) and their running min (right): a staircase that steps down each time a new record is set and holds level in between. A new random array comes every second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.maximum.accumulate holds the other extreme. np.amin gives just the final smallest value. t.cummin is the PyTorch version. accumulate keeps a running total of a stream.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 820,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Replaces each value with the smallest value seen up to that point: the running min. The result only ever falls or stays level - a trough hold.\n\nUse it for the lowest point reached so far, the best time so far, a floor that only ever drops. Applied to a whole recording at once, it shows at every moment the record so far.\n\nnan values are skipped: a gap does not reset the run.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.minimum.accumulate <axis: int>\nDefault: axis -1 (along each row).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe array (a list is turned into one). Every input is processed and sent.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way to run: -1 along each row, 0 down each column - one running min per joint for a buffer of frames x joints.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nrunning min:\nThe smallest value so far at each position, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n32 random values (left) and their running min (right): a staircase that steps down each time a new record is set and holds level in between. A new random array comes every second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.maximum.accumulate holds the other extreme. np.amin gives just the final smallest value. t.cummin is the PyTorch version. accumulate keeps a running total of a stream."
        },
